import Submission.Blueprint.Interfaces

/-!
# AREA REFL — solid objects are reflective

This is the base module of the REFL area (`Work.REFL`). It fixes the small family of
*test morphisms* `W` which characterizes solid objects, following RC Prop. 3.2.5:
`M` is solid iff for every light profinite `S` the map `((1−S) ⊗ id_{ℤ[S]})^*` on
`Hom(P ⊗ ℤ[S], M)` is bijective, because `(ihom P M)(S) = Hom(ℤ[S] ⊗ P, M)` and a map
of sheaves is an isomorphism iff it is bijective on all `S`-sections.

Both occurrences of `S` range over a *small* set of light profinite sets (a skeleton), which
is what makes `W` a small morphism property so that the orthogonal reflection construction
(Mathlib `MorphismProperty.isRightAdjoint_ι_isLocal`) applies.

## Contents

* `Work.REFL.TestIndex`, `Work.REFL.testSpace` — the small index set of light profinite sets;
* `Work.REFL.testMap S` — the endomorphism `(1 − S) ⊗ id_{ℤ[S]}` of `P ⊗ ℤ[S]`;
* `Work.REFL.solidW` — the small set of test morphisms;
* `Work.REFL.sections S` — the functor `M ↦ M(S)` on light condensed abelian groups;
* `Work.REFL.isSolid_iff_solidW` — `isSolid M ↔ solidW.isLocal M` (RC Prop 3.2.5).
-/

open CategoryTheory Limits LightProfinite LightCondensed MonoidalCategory MonoidalClosed

noncomputable section

namespace Work.REFL

open _root_.LightCondensed _root_.LightCondensed.Solid

/-- `ℵ₀` is regular (as an instance, for the statements of presentability below). -/
instance : Fact (Cardinal.aleph0).IsRegular := Cardinal.fact_isRegular_aleph0

/-! ## The small family of test morphisms -/

/-- A small skeleton of the category of light profinite sets: the index type of the test
morphisms. It is small (`Type`), while `LightProfinite` itself is only essentially small. -/
abbrev TestIndex : Type := SmallModel.{0} LightProfinite.{0}

/-- The light profinite set attached to a test index. -/
abbrev testSpace : TestIndex → LightProfinite.{0} := (equivSmallModel LightProfinite.{0}).inverse.obj

/-- The endomorphism `(1 − S) ⊗ id_{ℤ[S]}` of `P ⊗ ℤ[S]`, the "null-sequence test map". -/
noncomputable def testMap (S : LightProfinite.{0}) :
    P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed ⟶ P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed :=
  oneMinusShift ▷ (free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed

/-- The small family `W` of endomorphisms `id_{ℤ[S]} ⊗ (1 − S)` for `S` running through a
small skeleton of the light profinite sets. `M` is solid iff it is local for `W`. -/
noncomputable def solidW : MorphismProperty LightCondAb :=
  MorphismProperty.ofHoms (X := fun i : TestIndex => P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj (testSpace i).toCondensed)
    (Y := fun i : TestIndex => P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj (testSpace i).toCondensed)
    (fun i => testMap (testSpace i))

instance : solidW.IsSmall.{0} := MorphismProperty.isSmall_ofHoms _

/-- Unfolding of `solidW.isLocal`: a morphism property generated by a family is local for an
object iff each member of the family induces a bijection by precomposition. -/
theorem solidW_isLocal_iff (M : LightCondAb) :
    solidW.isLocal M ↔ ∀ i : TestIndex,
      Function.Bijective (fun g : P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj (testSpace i).toCondensed ⟶ M =>
        testMap (testSpace i) ≫ g) := by
  constructor
  · intro hM i
    exact hM (testMap (testSpace i)) (.mk i)
  · rintro hM X Y f ⟨i⟩
    exact hM i

/-! ## The sections functor -/

/-- The functor `M ↦ M(S)` of sections at a light profinite set, on light condensed abelian
groups. `sections S` is isomorphic to `Hom(ℤ[S], -)`. -/
abbrev sections (S : LightProfinite.{0}) : LightCondAb ⥤ ModuleCat.{0} ℤ :=
  sheafToPresheaf (coherentTopology LightProfinite.{0}) (ModuleCat.{0} ℤ) ⋙
    (evaluation LightProfiniteᵒᵖ (ModuleCat.{0} ℤ)).obj ⟨S⟩


/-! ## Shorthands used to state the intermediate results -/

/-- The internal hom `ihom P M`, as an object of `LightCondAb`. -/
abbrev ihomP (M : LightCondAb) : LightCondAb := (ihom P).obj M

/-- The endomorphism `pre oneMinusShift` of the functor `ihom P`, at the object `M`. -/
abbrev preShift (M : LightCondAb) : ihomP M ⟶ ihomP M :=
  (MonoidalClosed.pre oneMinusShift).app M

/-- The points of `ihom P M` at `S` are maps `P ⊗ ℤ[S] ⟶ M`. -/
abbrev ihomPPoints (M : LightCondAb) (S : LightProfinite.{0}) :
    (ihomP M).obj.obj ⟨S⟩ ≃ (P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed ⟶ M) :=
  LightCondensed.ihomPoints ℤ P M S

/-! ## The statements of the intermediate results

The statements below are the pieces of the proof of `refl_prop`, in dependency order.  They are
stated as `Prop`s so that they can be passed as hypotheses between the different modules of this
area. -/

/-- **Filtered colimits of sheaves are computed pointwise.** The pointwise (presheaf) colimit of a
filtered diagram of light condensed abelian groups is again a sheaf.

Detailed proof plan (all names are Mathlib names unless prefixed with `Work.REFL`).
Write `J = coherentTopology LightProfinite.{0}`, `D = ModuleCat.{0} ℤ`, `Q = c.pt`, so that
`Q(S)` is the colimit of the `F k (S)` (`IsColimit` for the evaluation functor, which preserves
colimits).
1. `Presheaf.IsSheaf J Q` unfolds (definitionally) to
   `∀ E : D, Presieve.IsSheaf J (Q ⋙ coyoneda.obj (op E))`.
2. `isSheaf_coherent` (Type-valued) reduces this to the sheaf condition for the presieves
   `Presieve.ofArrows X π` coming from *finite effective epi families*: it suffices to show
   `Presieve.IsSheafFor (Q ⋙ coyoneda.obj (op E)) (Presieve.ofArrows X π)`.
3. `Presheaf.forkMap`, `Presheaf.firstMap`, `Presheaf.secondMap`, `Presheaf.w` are defined for
   arbitrary target categories; `Presheaf.IsSheaf' J P` is the statement that the fork of `P` over
   every presieve generating a cover is a limit, and `Presheaf.isSheaf_iff_isSheaf'` shows
   `Presheaf.IsSheaf J P ↔ Presheaf.IsSheaf' J P`.  In particular each `F k` is a sheaf, hence
   `Nonempty (IsLimit (Fork.ofι (Presheaf.forkMap R (F k)) (Presheaf.w R (F k))))` for
   `R = Presieve.ofArrows X π` (using `Sieve.generate R ∈ J B`).
4. The fork is natural (functorial) in the presheaf variable: define functors
   `firstObjF R`, `secondObjF R : (LightProfiniteᵒᵖ ⥤ D) ⥤ D` with object maps
   `P ↦ Presheaf.firstObj R P`, `P ↦ Presheaf.secondObj R P` and the evident `Pi.lift`/`Pi.π`
   maps, and natural transformations `forkMapF R : (evaluation at B) ⟶ firstObjF R`,
   `firstMapF R secondMapF R : firstObjF R ⟶ secondObjF R`, whose components are
   `Presheaf.forkMap R P`, `Presheaf.firstMap R P`, `Presheaf.secondMap R P`.
5. Apply `Limits.colimitLimitIso` to the diagram `WalkingParallelPair ⥤ (K ⥤ D)` given by
   `parallelPair (firstMapF R).whiskerRight F (secondMapF R).whiskerRight F`: filtered colimits
   commute with finite limits (`PreservesFiniteLimits (colim : (K ⥤ D) ⥤ D)`, AB5 for modules, via
   `Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Limits.FilteredColimitCommutesFiniteLimit`).  Combining with step 3 this
   shows that the fork of `Q` over `R` is a limit
   (`IsLimit.ofIsoLimit` along the natural iso identifying
   `colim_k (Presheaf.firstObj R (F k)) ≅ Presheaf.firstObj R Q` and the corresponding maps).
6. For each `E`, `Presheaf.isSheafForIsSheafFor' Q (coyoneda.obj (op E)) B R` compares this
   limit to the limit of the *Type-valued* fork of `Q ⋙ coyoneda.obj (op E)`, and
   `Equalizer.Presieve.sheaf_condition` converts the latter into
   `Presieve.IsSheafFor (Q ⋙ coyoneda.obj (op E)) R`.
7. Steps 1, 2 and 6 conclude.

*Alternative plan via "germs"* (a third strategy, avoiding the functoriality of the fork).
Again it suffices to show that the fork of `Q` over `R = Presieve.ofArrows X π` (a finite family)
is a limit; convert that into `Presieve.IsSheafFor (Q ⋙ coyoneda.obj (op E)) R` by
`Presheaf.isSheafForIsSheafFor'` (with `s = coyoneda.obj (op E)`) followed by
`Equalizer.Presieve.sheaf_condition`, and then finish with `isSheaf_coherent` and the definition
of `Presheaf.IsSheaf`.  For the fork condition use the explicit description of the filtered
colimit of modules:
* `forget (ModuleCat ℤ)` preserves filtered colimits, so the type-level API of
  `CategoryTheory.Limits.Types` applies to `(evaluation S).mapCocone c`: every element of `Q(S)`
  is the image of an element of some `F k (S)` and an equality in the colimit already holds at
  some level;
* combine finitely many levels using filteredness (`IsFiltered`), and glue the corresponding
  compatible family in `F k` using that `F k` is a sheaf
  (`Presheaf.IsSheaf.existsUnique_amalgamation_ofArrows` or `Presheaf.isSheaf_iff_isSheaf'`).
This shows that `Presheaf.forkMap R Q` is a mono (germs of `0`) and that every equalizing
element of the product lifts; both are elementwise statements about modules, so they give that
the canonical map from `Q(U)` to the equalizer of `Presheaf.firstMap R Q`, `Presheaf.secondMap R Q`
is an isomorphism, whence the fork is a limit. -/
abbrev PointwiseColimitIsSheaf : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Type) [SmallCategory K] [IsFiltered K] (F : K ⥤ LightCondAb)
    (c : Cocone (F ⋙ sheafToPresheaf (coherentTopology LightProfinite.{0}) (ModuleCat.{0} ℤ))),
    IsColimit c → Presheaf.IsSheaf (coherentTopology LightProfinite.{0}) c.pt

/-- **Filtered colimits of light condensed abelian groups are computed sectionwise**: the functor
`M ↦ M(S)` preserves filtered colimits.

Proof plan.  From `PointwiseColimitIsSheaf`, `Sheaf.createsColimitOfIsSheaf` gives
`CreatesColimit F (sheafToPresheaf J D)` for every filtered diagram `F`; since the presheaf
category has colimits, `preservesColimit_of_createsColimit_and_hasColimit` gives
`PreservesColimit F (sheafToPresheaf J D)`.  Finally `sections S` is
`sheafToPresheaf J D ⋙ evaluation _ _ ⟨S⟩`, evaluation preserves colimits, and the sections of
the (presheaf) colimit are computed pointwise. -/
abbrev SectionsFiltered : Prop :=
  ∀ (J : Type) [SmallCategory J] [IsFiltered J] (S : LightProfinite.{0}),
    PreservesColimitsOfShape J (sections S)

/-- **The free objects `ℤ[S]` are finitely presentable** (`ℵ₀`-presentable, i.e. compact).

Proof plan.  `IsCardinalPresentable X ℵ₀` unfolds to `(coyoneda.obj (op X)).IsCardinalAccessible ℵ₀`,
i.e. `Hom(ℤ[S], -)` preserves colimits indexed by an `IsCardinalFiltered J ℵ₀` shape; since
`isFiltered_of_isCardinalFiltered` turns this into `IsFiltered J`, the hypothesis `SectionsFiltered`
applies.

The bridge is the natural bijection `Hom(ℤ[S], M) ≅ M(S)`: combine
* `(LightCondensed.freeForgetAdjunction ℤ).homEquiv S.toCondensed M : ((free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed ⟶ M) ≃ (S.toCondensed ⟶ (LightCondensed.forget ℤ).obj M)`
  (natural in `M` by `Adjunction.homEquiv_naturality_right`),
* `(coherentTopology LightProfinite.{0}).yonedaEquiv : ((coherentTopology _).yoneda.obj S ⟶ F) ≃ F.obj.obj (op S)`,
  where `(coherentTopology _).yoneda.obj S = S.toCondensed` by `rfl` (natural in `F` by
  `GrothendieckTopology.yonedaEquiv_comp`),
* `Equiv.ulift.symm : α ≃ ULift.{1,0} α` (the codomain `coyoneda.obj (op X)` lands in `Type 1`,
  while `M ↦ M(S)` lands in `Type 0`; use `uliftFunctor.{1, 0}` or the abbreviation
  `(sections S ⋙ forget (ModuleCat.{0} ℤ)) ⋙ uliftFunctor.{1, 0}`, which is reducible, to keep the
  universe levels aligned).

Build this as `NatIso.ofComponents (fun M => Equiv.toIso ...) _`; for the naturality square use
`Adjunction.homEquiv_naturality_right` and `GrothendieckTopology.yonedaEquiv_comp`
(`Type`-valued morphisms are `ULift`-wrapped functions, so `ext`/`ConcreteCategory`-style
extensionality may be needed).  Then transport `PreservesColimitsOfShape J (sections S)` along the
inverse iso with `preservesColimitsOfShape_of_natIso`, using
`comp_preservesColimitsOfShape` to compose with `forget (ModuleCat.{0} ℤ)` and `uliftFunctor`
(both preserve filtered colimits). -/
abbrev AtomsPresentable : Prop :=
  ∀ (S : LightProfinite.{0}), IsCardinalPresentable ((free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed) (Cardinal.aleph0 : Cardinal.{0})

/-- **The test objects `P ⊗ ℤ[S]` are finitely presentable.**

Proof plan.  `P` is a retract of `ℤ[ℕ∪∞]` (`LightCondensed.P_retract`), so `P ⊗ ℤ[S]` is a
retract of `ℤ[ℕ∪∞] ⊗ ℤ[S] ≅ ℤ[(ℕ∪∞) × S]`.  The isomorphism is the monoidality of the free
functor: Mathlib knows `Functor.Monoidal (LightCondensed.free ℤ)`, so `μ` gives
`ℤ[A] ⊗ ℤ[B] ≅ ℤ[A ⊗ B]`, and `LightCondSet` is cartesian monoidal.  `IsCardinalPresentable` is
closed under retracts (`Mathlib.CategoryTheory/Presentable/Retracts.lean`) and under isomorphisms,
so `AtomsPresentable` applies. -/
abbrev TestObjectsPresentable : Prop :=
  ∀ (S : LightProfinite.{0}),
    IsCardinalPresentable (P ⊗ (free ℤ).obj S.toCondensed) (Cardinal.aleph0 : Cardinal.{0})

/-- The component description of `pre oneMinusShift` on `S`-sections (see `pre_app_points`).

Proof plan: `MonoidalClosed.curry_pre_app` states `curry g ≫ (pre f).app M = curry (f ▷ Y ≫ g)`;
combine it with the naturality of `GrothendieckTopology.yonedaEquiv` and of the free-forget
adjunction, as in the proof of `LightCondensed.ihom_map_val_app`. -/
abbrev PrePointsStatement : Prop :=
  ∀ (M : LightCondAb) (S : LightProfinite.{0}) (x : (ihomP M).obj.obj (Opposite.op S)),
    ((preShift M).hom.app (Opposite.op S)) x
      = (ihomPPoints M S).symm (testMap S ≫ (ihomPPoints M S) x)

/-- The local-object form of solidity: `M` is solid iff it is local for the small family
`solidW` of test morphisms (RC Prop. 3.2.5).

Proof plan.  `isSolid M = IsIso (preShift M)`, which by `NatTrans.isIso_iff_isIso_app` holds iff
every component is an isomorphism, i.e. iff for every `S` the component at the `S`-sections is
bijective.  By the points lemma and the equivalence `ihomPPoints M S`, that is precisely
bijectivity of precomposition with `testMap S` on `Hom(P ⊗ ℤ[S], M)`.  `solidW_isLocal_iff` says
that `solidW.isLocal M` is the same condition restricted to the small skeleton `testSpace`;
the two are equivalent because every light profinite set is isomorphic to some `testSpace i`
(`equivSmallModel LightProfinite.{0}`) and the conditions are transported by naturality. -/
abbrev IsSolidIsLocalStatement : Prop := ∀ M : LightCondAb, isSolid M ↔ solidW.isLocal M

end Work.REFL
